# Three essays chosen by Ruslan

**E050, E012 and E051.** This is the human choice recorded after the AI coordinator's earlier recommendation. The links below open the preserved working texts covered by the accepted checks. Final revisions and the last repository update will be commissioned separately.

## E050 · What Holding Cannot Tell Us About Letting Go

**Question:** Can holding a system steady tell us what happens when we let go?

Two systems agree on an extensive collection of permanent holding outcomes but diverge after release. Within the specified model family, a staged release converges to the intended equilibrium. The result depends on the stated family, preparation and time scale; it does not promise a common finite completion time across all parameter values.

[Read the working text](../essays/selection-20261018/E050.md) · [Development](../essays/E050/README.md)

## E012 · When a Remedy Becomes Evidence

**Question:** Can paying compensation leave an obligation unfinished?

A fund destroys photographs it promised to keep and compensates the loss. Later, another body falsely treats the claim as fabricated. If the photographs would have supplied an inexpensive defence, the first fund may still owe reasonable help. The revised example also shows when missing photographs would make no difference.

[Read the working text](../essays/selection-20261018/E012.md) · [Development](../essays/E012/README.md) · [A revision followed step by step](worked-example.md)

## E051 · Difference without Reconstruction

**Question:** Can memory reveal a difference without reproducing an earlier experience?

A comparison may preserve a relation between experiences without recreating either one. In the essay's deterministic model, a comparison can lose information yet still reveal a difference; matching outputs need an additional condition to establish sameness. This pattern does not establish that actual human memory satisfies those conditions.

[Read the working text](../essays/E051/original.md) · [Development and new checks](../essays/E051/README.md)

## How this choice differs from the earlier recommendation

The coordinator recommended **E050, E009 and E012**, with **E078 and E041** in reserve, after twenty checks of the ten reading candidates. Ruslan then chose E051 as the third essay. E051 had been outside both the twenty-two-candidate pool and the ten-text reading selection. Its return is a separate branch in the [selection tree](selection-tree.md), not a retrospective change to those decisions.

Two new E051 reviews joined the four already accepted E050/E012 reviews. All six reported no major defect. The [accepted plan](../evidence/selection-update/20261101/REVISION_PLAN.json) still calls for precise comparisons with close predecessors and a few clarifications. These reviewers assessed the frozen essays, not later revisions or the coordinator's subsequent plan.

